# Copy to .env next to compose.yaml (never commit .env).
# Docker Compose reads this file and passes values into the containers.

LLM_PROVIDER=claude

# Per-role model + reasoning (Claude provider; the local provider is tuned via LOCAL_* below).
# Format: model:effort  |  effort-only  |  model-only
# Defaults: planner=opus:high  implementer=sonnet:medium  verify=sonnet:low  ask=sonnet:low
# Any Claude model id works, e.g. claude-fable-5 (premium tier: thinking always on,
# effort low..max, longer turns — raise CLAUDE_AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS when using it):
# PLANNER_REASONING=claude-fable-5:high
# IMPLEMENTER_REASONING=claude-fable-5:medium
# PLANNER_REASONING=opus:high
# IMPLEMENTER_REASONING=sonnet:medium
# VERIFY_REASONING=sonnet:low
# ASK_REASONING=sonnet:low

# Credentials come ONLY from .env — set ONE Claude credential.
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN is PREFERRED (subscription token from `claude setup-token`):
# smaller blast radius (subscription rate-limit, not open billing) and easy to revoke.
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=              # fallback if you have no subscription token
# Under Docker these are held by the egress-gw custody proxy, never the worker: compose
# points ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL at the proxy and gives the worker a placeholder, so the real
# token stays out of the agent's reach. Nothing to set for ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL here.

# ── Local model (LLM_PROVIDER=local) — Ollama / any OpenAI-compatible endpoint ──
# No cloud key: the "credential" is just a reachable endpoint, so auth is NOT
# fail-closed and no remote spend is possible. Switch models (Qwen↔Mistral↔Llama)
# by changing LOCAL_MODEL only — the model is config, not code.
#   1) on the host:  OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0:11434 ollama serve   (0.0.0.0 so containers reach it)
#   2) ollama pull qwen2.5-coder:32b        # or :7b on modest / CPU-only hardware
#   3) set LLM_PROVIDER=local above, then pick a layout:
#
# A) harness in Docker + Ollama on the same VM/host — one constant on every platform,
#    reached through the gateway's fixed loopback forwarder:
# LOCAL_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1
# EGRESS_ALLOW_HOST_DOCKER_INTERNAL=1     # let default-deny egress reach the host gateway IP
# B) harness on the host (npm run dev:cli) + Ollama on the host — egress-gw not involved:
# LOCAL_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
# Model on a DIFFERENT host? Set LOCAL_UPSTREAM_HOST=<ip> (the forwarder targets it) and
# EGRESS_ALLOW_CIDRS=<ip>/32; LOCAL_BASE_URL stays http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1.
#
# LOCAL_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:32b
# LOCAL_API_KEY=                            # optional; Ollama ignores it
# Optional per-role models + tuning:
# LOCAL_PLANNER_MODEL=
# LOCAL_IMPLEMENTER_MODEL=
# LOCAL_TIMEOUT_MS=600000        # whole-run wall-clock budget
# LOCAL_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=300000  # per request; raise for big models — the FIRST call
#                                  # pays a cold model-load cost (a 32B model is ~20GB)
# LOCAL_MAX_TURNS=40
# LOCAL_CONTEXT_TOKENS=24000     # soft prompt-token budget; the loop evicts oldest tool
#                                # rounds to fit. Raise it for long-context models (set
#                                # near the real window); 0 disables compaction.
# LOCAL_TEMPERATURE=0

# Telegram — required for tg-bot (`docker compose --profile tg-bot up -d tg-bot`)
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN is held by the egress-gw custody proxy under Docker (the worker gets a
# placeholder + TELEGRAM_API_BASE pointing at the proxy). TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_IDS gates
# INBOUND authors — who may message the bot (silent drop otherwise).
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_IDS=123456789
# OUTBOUND recipient pinning: the proxy rejects any Bot API call whose chat_id/from_chat_id
# is outside this list, for ANY method (sendMessage, sendDocument, forwardMessage, …),
# fail-closed. In 1:1 chats this equals the author id above; in a group it is the (negative)
# group chat id. tg-bot also needs EGRESS_ALLOW_TELEGRAM=1 (see the egress section).
TELEGRAM_RECIPIENT_IDS=123456789

# HTTP handle — used by the `http` service (docker compose --profile http up -d http).
# HARNESS_HTTP_TOKEN is empty by default → POST /goal is OPEN to anyone who can
# reach the port. The port is only published on the host loopback by default, but
# set a token before exposing it on any shared/remote network.
HARNESS_HTTP_PORT=8080
# Published on 127.0.0.1 by default. To reach it from another host, set
# HARNESS_HTTP_BIND=0.0.0.0 AND a token below.
# HARNESS_HTTP_BIND=127.0.0.1
# HARNESS_HTTP_TOKEN=change-me

AGENTS=auto
# Role briefs (lib/agents/). compose bind-mounts ./lib/agents by default — edit on the
# host, restart the service (no image rebuild). Override host or in-container paths.
# The host override may be any native absolute path (spaces and Unicode are fine);
# a relative value resolves against the demo dir. Long-form binds carry it verbatim.
# HARNESS_AGENTS_HOST_DIR=./lib/agents
# HARNESS_AGENTS_DIR=/app/repovine/examples/agent-harness-demo/lib/agents
# Planner rule dir (write-only sub-agent tasks). Default in compose: lib/rules/
# HARNESS_RULES_DIR=/app/repovine/examples/agent-harness-demo/lib/rules
# Inter-sub-agent handoff (markdown), demo-owned: lib/handoff/handoff.md
# HARNESS_HANDOFF_DIR=/app/repovine/examples/agent-harness-demo/lib/handoff
# /ask read-only agent (MCP + file reads). Default timeout 600s.
# ASK_TIMEOUT_MS=600000
# ASK_MAX_TURNS=24

# Where the agent's sandbox is bind-mounted ON THE HOST (gitignored). Inspect the
# files the agent created under <dir>/sandbox/. Default ./.workspace next to compose.yaml.
# Any native absolute path works (spaces and Unicode are fine); a relative value
# resolves against the demo dir. scripts/compose.mjs pre-creates the dir before compose.
# HARNESS_WORKSPACE_DIR=./.workspace

# Resource ceilings (per container) so a runaway agent/build can't exhaust the host.
HARNESS_MEM_LIMIT=4g
HARNESS_CPUS=2
# Node heap ceiling inside the harness app container.
# NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=4096
# The Claude Code SDK writes config/caches under $HOME. Default points at the workspace
# bind mount (host disk) so multi-agent runs don't fill the container's tiny tmpfs (ENOSPC).
# HARNESS_HOME=/app/repovine/examples/agent-harness-demo/.workspace/.harness-home
# HARNESS_NODE_TMPFS=512m
# HARNESS_TMP_TMPFS=512m
# Telegram/HTTP reply detail: default is compact step summary; set 1 for file/contract lists.
# HARNESS_REPLY_VERBOSE=1
# Harness logs include app-side ISO timestamps by default; set 0 for legacy terse lines.
# HARNESS_LOG_TIMESTAMPS=0
# Greenfield runs fan scaffold implement roles out in parallel git worktrees (merged at
# join; conflicts surface as blockers). Set 0 to force sequential. Continuation runs are
# always sequential — the inter-stage refresh barrier carries contracts between roles.
# HARNESS_GREENFIELD_PARALLEL=0
# Advisory LLM review of the planner's plan: one bounded call, findings must cite an
# orient claim/node (uncited ones are discarded), logged + recorded in plan_finished,
# never a veto. Recommended when attached to a real repository; sandbox default off.
# HARNESS_PLAN_REVIEW=1

# ── Egress allow-list (egress-gw sidecar) ─────────────────────────────────────
# The harness can reach ONLY these domains (+ subdomains); everything else is
# dropped (fail-closed). Watch for blocked lookups:  docker compose logs egress-gw
# claude reaches anthropic.com (the origin proxy re-originates there). api.telegram.org
# is NOT here — it opens only via EGRESS_ALLOW_TELEGRAM below. local needs no domain
# (it uses EGRESS_ALLOW_HOST_DOCKER_INTERNAL / EGRESS_ALLOW_CIDRS for the host model).
ALLOW_DOMAINS=anthropic.com
# To let runtime_check npm install / build-test the generated project, widen this (and set
# HARNESS_RUNTIME_CHECK=1 below):
# ALLOW_DOMAINS=anthropic.com,registry.npmjs.org,api.github.com,objects.githubusercontent.com
# tg-bot profile: set 1 to add api.telegram.org to the allow-list. The proxy still pins
# every outbound chat_id/from_chat_id to TELEGRAM_RECIPIENT_IDS, so the open domain does
# not let the agent message an arbitrary chat.
EGRESS_ALLOW_TELEGRAM=0
# Upstream resolver the gateway forwards to (must be reachable on :53). If your
# host forces an internal DNS, set it here.
UPSTREAM_DNS=1.1.1.1
# Gateway startup/healthcheck probes: anthropic.com must pass, example.com must fail.
# Set EGRESS_SELF_TEST=0 to skip the network probes while keeping process/ipset health.
# EGRESS_SELF_TEST=1
# EGRESS_SELF_TEST_ALLOW_URLS=
# EGRESS_SELF_TEST_BLOCKED_URL=https://example.com/

# ── Worker sandbox: the container IS the sandbox ──────────────────────────────
# Confinement comes from the container, not an in-process sandbox: default-deny
# egress (network off from executed commands), a read-only rootfs with a write-only
# workspace bind mount, cap_drop:ALL, and no Docker-in-Docker. The prompt also tells
# the agent to write code, not run it.
#
# The Claude Agent SDK's own bwrap sandbox is fail-hard (failIfUnavailable), and bwrap
# CANNOT start in this container — unprivileged user namespaces are disabled — so it
# must be OFF here or the claude worker aborts at startup. Setting CLAUDE_SANDBOX=0 is
# what starts claude in Docker, not a relaxation of the posture above.
CLAUDE_SANDBOX=0

# ── runtime_check (orchestrator builds/tests the generated project in the sandbox) ──
# OFF by default: the hardened egress allows only anthropic.com, so `npm install` and
# `npm run build && npm test` fail closed. Sealed posture — the agent WRITES the code and
# you build/verify it on the host. To let the HARNESS build/test instead, set this to 1
# AND open npm (+ github release hosts) in ALLOW_DOMAINS above.
# ATTACH MODE forces this off regardless of the value: it would build/test the whole real
# repo after every episode and fail closed under the locked egress; an explicit =1 is reported
# as overridden in the intake log. Build/test an attached repo on the host after promotion.
HARNESS_RUNTIME_CHECK=0
# When enabled: installs deps `npm install --ignore-scripts` in each package dir missing
# node_modules, then runs build+test (scrubbed env — no provider keys):
# HARNESS_RUNTIME_CHECK_CMD="npm run build && npm test"
# HARNESS_RUNTIME_CHECK_CMDS=npm run build; npm test
# HARNESS_RUNTIME_CHECK_FIX=0                # auto-fix sub-agent after failure; on unless =0
# HARNESS_RUNTIME_CHECK_FIX_MAX=2            # per failing command
# HARNESS_RUNTIME_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS=600000
# HARNESS_RUNTIME_CHECK_LOG_TAIL=40

# ── Attach mode (run against an existing repository worktree) ─────────────────
# HARNESS_TARGET_MODE selects the target: sandbox (default — managed workspace
# under .workspace/sandbox) or attach (pre-created host Git worktree mounted at
# /workspace in the container). Leave it unset here: the attach compose services
# pin attach themselves, and the launcher forwards it on `run`.
# HARNESS_TARGET_MODE=sandbox
#
# The four vars below are HOST-side inputs for scripts/attach-launcher.mjs; they
# are never forwarded into the worker env. They are host paths — inside the
# container the worktree is always /workspace. Leave HARNESS_ATTACH_WORKTREE and
# HARNESS_ATTACH_STATE_DIR unset here: compose.attach.yaml marks them required
# (`${VAR:?}`), and the launcher sets them from the prepared launch on every call.
#
# NEVER point HARNESS_ATTACH_WORKTREE at the real checkout. Prepare a disposable
# LINKED worktree (`git -C <repo> worktree add <path>`); preflight rejects a
# primary checkout (.git directory) and a dirty tree. Absolute path.
# HARNESS_ATTACH_WORKTREE=/home/me/worktrees/myrepo-run-001
# Dedicated, existing, EMPTY dir — one per launch. The launcher creates control/
# and runtime/ inside it, fail-on-exist; relaunching needs a fresh empty dir.
# runtime/git is the launch-scoped disposable Git repo: one baseline commit of
# the worktree tree, tagged attach-baseline; /workspace/.git in the container is
# a read-only pointer into it. /reset restores that baseline (confirm-gated);
# committed attach/run-* branches survive a reset.
# HARNESS_ATTACH_STATE_DIR=/home/me/attach-state/run-001
# The attach container runs as this uid:gid, so writes land with the operator's
# ownership. Numeric (`id -u` / `id -g`); must own the worktree and the state dir.
# HARNESS_HOST_UID=1000
# HARNESS_HOST_GID=1000
#
# Where `prepare` places launches. Default: next to the checkout
# (<parent>/.repovine-harness-worktrees/ and <parent>/.repovine-harness-state/). Set the root to
# place them under <root>/worktrees/ and <root>/state/ instead.
# HARNESS_ATTACH_ROOT=/home/me/attach-launches
#
# Launch through the launcher (fail-closed preflight before any docker call):
#   npm run attach -- prepare <checkout>   # worktree + empty state from a checkout path; prints exports
#   npm run attach -- preflight            # validate only, creates nothing
#   npm run attach -- run cli --goal "…"   # or: run http | run tg-bot
# Promote a successful run (the reply names the branch: 📦 attach/run-<id> @ <oid>):
#   git -C <checkout> fetch <state-dir>/runtime/git attach/run-<id>:attach/run-<id>
#   git -C <checkout> merge attach/run-<id>
# Teardown is manual, in this order (fetch first — the state dir holds the repo):
# docker compose --profile attach-<cmd> down, then remove the state dir and the
# worktree (git worktree remove).
